# Production accessibilityIdentifier expression snapshot. Intentional changes
# require updating this file in the same reviewed change.
#
# 2026-08-10, Wave 1: snapshot the exact expression set rather than a multiset.
# Package extraction can temporarily leave a source copy outside compiler
# membership, and counting duplicates made an ownership move look like an
# identifier change. The 753-expression set is byte-identical between refactor
# HEAD and this remediation; only duplicate occurrences changed.
#
# 2026-08-10, UI polish Phase 2: 753 -> 757. Four additions, zero removals, all
# from the new shared primitives in LifeBoardUI:
#
#   "lifeboard.receipt", "lifeboard.receipt.undo", "lifeboard.receipt.dismiss"
#   "lifeboard.signal.\(item.id)"
#
# The signal identifier is interpolated on purpose. The checker records the
# expression text, so one interpolated identifier is one snapshot entry no
# matter how many signals are rendered — which is what keeps this gate stable
# as the primitives get adopted across screens. Adopting `ReceiptBar` in place
# of a hand-rolled receipt will *remove* that screen's bespoke identifiers, so
# expect the count to fall again during adoption.
#
# 2026-08-10, UI polish Phases 3-5: 757 -> 774. Additions only, no removals:
#
#   focus.surface{,.close,.commitment,.dial,.transport,.pauseResume,.nextPhase,
#                 .endSession,.end.\(outcome.rawValue),.commit,.commit.cancel,
#                 .interruption{,.strip,.reason}}   — the new Focus surface
#   lifeThread.composer.orb                          — the composer's compressed shape
#   lifeboard.decision{,.alternative}                — DecisionSurface
#
# `focus.session.\(session.id.uuidString)` is unchanged: the route kept its
# identifier when its content was replaced, because the identifier — not the
# view type — is what the UI-test contract is written against.
#
# 2026-08-10, adoption: 774 -> 775. One addition, `lifeboard.receipt.destination`,
# from extending `ReceiptBar` with an optional "go look at it" action so Plan's
# project-template receipt could adopt the shared bar without losing its Open
# button. `plan.projectTemplates.receipt` is unchanged — the screen kept its
# identifier while its implementation was replaced, which is the contract.
#
# `InsightsHeaderView.swift` was deleted this session and removed nothing here:
# it declared no identifiers, which is part of why it was the one file safe to
# delete without running the UI suite.
#
# 2026-08-10, signal rings restored: 774 -> 775. `"home.signal.\(slot.id)"` is
# back as a literal in `HomeSignalRow.ring(_:)`, so this gate covers Home's
# signal identifiers again — briefly it did not, because the SignalStrip
# adoption emitted them through an `identifierPrefix` parameter the checker
# cannot resolve. `"\(identifierPrefix).\(item.id)"` remains in the set:
# `SignalStrip` is kept in LifeBoardUI for future use, just no longer adopted
# on Home.
# 2026-08-14, Life Map onboarding: 775 -> 845. Additions only, no removals.
#
# The Life Map flow shipped with four raw string literals and no per-step or
# per-choice identifiers at all, so every UI assertion against it had to reach
# for a localized label. Labels are not a test contract: they move with copy
# edits and break under RTL and non-English runs. `LifeMapAccessibilityID` now
# covers all eleven steps plus the controls, and derives `step(_:)` from
# `LifeMapOnboardingStep` so a new step cannot be added without one.
#
# `identifierSuffix` is spelled out per case rather than derived from
# `String(describing:)` on purpose — the latter would silently rename every
# identifier if a case were renamed, turning a source refactor into a broken
# UI-test contract with no compiler error.
#
# 2026-08-14, dead nine-step flow removed: 845 -> 818. Removals only, zero
# additions — the 27 that went are exactly the `AppOnboardingAccessibilityID.*`
# entries plus the three interpolated day-shape/permission identifiers that only
# `AppOnboardingJourneyView` declared.
#
# The removal was diffed before deleting, per the standing hazard that dead UI
# stays reachable by identifier at runtime even with zero Swift references. Two
# live, non-onboarding UI test files — `Base/BaseUITest.swift` and
# `AppStoreScreenshotUITests.swift` — do still reference the *test-side*
# `AccessibilityIdentifiers.Onboarding` constants. They keep compiling (those are
# test-target string constants with no app dependency), but they now match
# nothing at runtime. That was already true before this deletion: the Life Map
# flow has been the presented flow for a while and never declared
# `onboarding.flow`. Rewiring those two files to the `LifeMapAccessibilityID`
# namespace is the follow-up.
# 2026-08-18, Eva chat escape hatches: 818 -> 820. Three additions, one removal.
#
#   + "chat.composer.dismiss_keyboard"  keyboard accessory Done on Eva's composer
#   + "eva.chat.back"                   back-to-Home control on Eva's top strip
#   + "eva.privacy.badge"               the collapsed privacy disclosure
#   - "chat.activation_starter.\(prompt.id)"
#
# The removal is a move, not a loss: the activation starters left the empty
# state's centred carousel for the composer's own rail, where they already
# carried "chat.activation.composer_starter.\(prompt.id)". The three additions
# are the controls that make Eva escapable — the shell hides the compact dock
# while Eva's composer is focused, so with the keyboard up there was previously
# no control on screen that could dismiss it or leave the screen.

# 2026-08-19, EVA onboarding merged into app onboarding. Removals:
# `LifeMapAccessibilityID.step(.permissionsPowerUp)` and `.step(.evaPowerUp)` —
# both steps are gone, replaced by `.step(.calendar)`, `.step(.health)`,
# `.step(.reminders)`, `.step(.eva)`, plus the closing `.step(.tour)` and
# `.step(.firstWin)`. `LifeMapAccessibilityID.permission(kind.id)` and
# `permissionSkip(kind.id)` are replaced by concrete-kind spellings; the
# *resolved* values (`onboarding.permission.calendar`, ...) are unchanged, so the
# UI-test contract in `AccessibilityIdentifiers.Onboarding.permission(_:)` still
# matches. Additions cover the calendar scoping list, the Health write-back
# toggles, EVA's consent rows, and the staged first-win prompts.

count=838
